WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.620
I’ve always wanted to share the stage with
a creepy skull mannequin, by the way.

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:05.069
That’s just fantastic.

00:00:05.069 --> 00:00:07.609
When I was younger,

00:00:07.609 --> 00:00:11.800
although not quite so young that I should
really not have known better,

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:15.430
I sent one of the most harsh messages I've
ever sent in my life.

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:16.630
I'm going to share it with you now.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:18.939
I'm not proud of it, but here it is:

00:00:18.939 --> 00:00:21.189
“I've blocked you everywhere else,

00:00:21.189 --> 00:00:23.980
“and now I'm going to block you here too.

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:28.940
“My only regret is that I can’t
block you in real life.”

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:31.820
Past me was a dick.

00:00:31.829 --> 00:00:34.290
I couldn’t block them in real life, right?

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:36.580
Because they were still in the
same social circles,

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:38.400
They were still going to get invited to the
same events,

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:39.730
still going to be part of the same things,

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:41.830
and it’s going to be awkward,

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:45.290
particularly after I send a message like that.

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:48.290
And some folks would say that I should just
get used to it,

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:52.120
that I should just be at the peace
with the fact that some people are just jerks,

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:54.450
and some personalities clash, but I thought,

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:58.940
what if instead:
we use technology to fix that.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:05.590
So my proposal for you tonight is:
blocking in real life.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:07.200
Which has been done in science fiction, right?

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.870
This is from Black Mirror.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:11.060
But the trouble with science fiction blocking

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:12.890
is that they have to make it work on camera,

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:18.360
and that means inevitably it’s a messy fuzz
of pixels or something like that,

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.469
and that’s not useful, that’s not helpful.

00:01:20.469 --> 00:01:23.159
Because a messy block of pixels can still
see you,

00:01:23.159 --> 00:01:24.159
and you can still see it,

00:01:24.159 --> 00:01:26.079
and it can still punch you in the face.

00:01:26.079 --> 00:01:29.369
So that’s not great.

00:01:29.369 --> 00:01:32.580
But we do have an algorithm for this that
will already work,

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.749
and Photoshop has it, it’s called
content-aware fill,

00:01:34.749 --> 00:01:36.009
and it’s the…

00:01:36.009 --> 00:01:39.179
“Woo!” from a Photoshop user there!

00:01:39.179 --> 00:01:40.179
Although given this audience,

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.520
possibly the man who created the feature,
we don’t know.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.600
I was actually originally in that photograph.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:48.049
There I am, that’s the original.

00:01:48.049 --> 00:01:50.490
But nowadays it’s trivial to just find an
object,

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:52.350
paint it out, and there you go.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:54.320
And if you didn’t have those
two identical bushes

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:55.570
right there in the middle,

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:58.279
you might not even spot that I was never there
in the first place.

00:01:58.279 --> 00:02:01.619
Now, Facebook already has facial recognition
for people, right?

00:02:01.619 --> 00:02:04.929
So in theory, you get a photo… here’s
one of me and some friends.

00:02:04.929 --> 00:02:06.009
You get a photo,

00:02:06.009 --> 00:02:07.819
you could highlight the person,

00:02:07.819 --> 00:02:08.909
and you could paint them…

00:02:08.909 --> 00:02:10.830
Yes, well it doesn’t…

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:14.080
It does go a little bit Cronenberg sometimes.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:20.140
But in the next few decades we’re going
to figure out how to make

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:22.840
much, much deeper connections into the brain.

00:02:22.849 --> 00:02:26.000
We’re already starting to see some things
like this.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.760
We've been using deep brain stimulation for
Parkinson’s patients since 2006,

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:32.430
that’s when that paper was from,

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.760
and spoiler, or as they insist
on calling it here,

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:36.220
“abstract”…

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:46.140
Abstract: it works.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:50.659
And we've already got people controlling
robot arms with brain implants,

00:02:50.659 --> 00:02:52.629
and exoskeletons with EEG caps.

00:02:52.629 --> 00:02:55.230
Okay, so no-one is actually working
on a neural lace yet…

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:58.780
No, of course Elon Musk is working
on a neural lace,

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:02.849
because Elon Musk is working on everything
that Iain M Banks ever wrote about.

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:05.340
So let’s say that it works.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.360
Let’s say that in the next few decades,

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:08.700
over the rest of the century,

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:12.790
we get something that starts to make deeper
and deeper connections into the brain.

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:14.420
And to start off with it’s going to help

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:17.489
an increasingly-aging population to deal with
senescence,

00:03:17.489 --> 00:03:20.050
but eventually it will trickle out to the
rest of us.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:24.069
And I'm willing to bet that humanity won’t
regress into virtual worlds,

00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:27.730
because virtual worlds are good
for gimmicky games, but they're not real.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:29.870
There’s no real human interaction there.

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:32.569
And sure, the uncanny valley has pretty much
been solved, right?

00:03:32.569 --> 00:03:37.590
That is Johnny Depp playing young Johnny Depp
in the latest Pirates movie.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:39.770
But you can’t have that for human interaction.

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:43.879
I'm willing to bet there’s the same
uncanny valley effect for talking to someone.

00:03:43.879 --> 00:03:49.719
You can’t simulate a human without simulating
something that’s very close to human,

00:03:49.719 --> 00:03:52.590
and that would be unethical,
even if it was possible.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:55.810
But you could hijack the vision centre.

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:58.769
You could use all those same
little tips and tricks

00:03:58.769 --> 00:04:03.709
that the brain has been using for,
well, millions of years of evolution,

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:07.050
to just quietly make you not notice things.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:09.020
If you walked here like this,

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:13.600
and quietly routed yourself around an obstacle
without even seeing it,

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:15.750
you're already using those tricks.

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:17.520
And yes, I have footnotes.

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:24.260
You could just tell your vision centre to
quietly remove things, or add things in.

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:26.760
You could teleport to a remote office by saying
to your implant,

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:29.530
or whatever it is, you want to go there.

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:31.180
It’d paint your vision with that,

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:34.280
and it would paint you into the vision of
the people there.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:36.470
But if you can do that,

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:39.050
then you can start to remove things as well.

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:43.000
Those implants could use the sort of hacks
that we've talked about

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:47.450
to quietly remove objects,
and people, and anything.

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:49.420
To start off with, this would just be used
on annoyances.

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:52.020
It'd be used on the baby that’s crying on
the plane,

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:55.889
it'd be used on the jerk who keeps leering
at you at the bus stop every day.

00:04:55.889 --> 00:04:57.979
But eventually it'd start
to roll out and roll out,

00:04:57.979 --> 00:05:00.020
you’d be able to block someone.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:01.300
You won’t appear in their perceptions,

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:03.610
and they won’t appear in yours.

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:06.630
And sure, there are going to be people
out there without implants,

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:09.270
but increasingly, as it becomes more and more
normalised,

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:10.770
like smartphones are today,

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:13.750
they're going to find themselves in a kind
of a twilight zone

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:15.970
where a lot of people are ignoring them.

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:19.300
The same way that people get better
customer service by tweeting someone

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:21.419
than they do by calling on a landline.

00:05:21.419 --> 00:05:24.860
And okay, there are people who are going to
try and get around this, right?

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:28.180
There are going to be moments of
human ingenuity that…

00:05:33.389 --> 00:05:36.100
It was really expensive to get that photo
made, by the way,

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:37.830
just the skywriting costs alone.

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:40.380
But in general,
people would be okay with this,

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:44.860
because no-one here could tell me the last
person who unfriended them on Facebook,

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:45.860
not with certainty.

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:47.699
Because you can’t remember everyone you’re
friends with,

00:05:47.699 --> 00:05:50.300
and you don’t check every day,
and it doesn’t notify you,

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:53.370
and it’s not particularly offensive
when it happens.

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:54.759
They're just not appearing anymore,

00:05:54.759 --> 00:05:57.160
you’ve just forgotten about them.

00:05:57.160 --> 00:05:58.599
So once we’re happy with that,

00:05:58.599 --> 00:06:01.410
the technology can start getting
better, and better, and better.

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:04.220
It can start blocking objects and concepts
and ideas,

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:06.690
and it can start to just
automatically work out the things

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:11.099
that your brain flinches away from
and automatically start to block them.

00:06:11.099 --> 00:06:13.230
And people will start sharing block lists,
right?

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:14.230
It won’t be monolithic,

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:16.300
it won’t be brought in from on high,

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:19.570
but you will just tend to block the same things
your friends block,

00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:22.210
because if you don’t, you keep noticing
the elephant in the room,

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:24.280
and you keep being the awkward one at the
dinner party

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:27.500
who keeps talking about things that people
don’t understand.

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:29.380
Or alternatively your friend is,

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:31.240
and will you please shut up about that, Dave,

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:33.319
we don’t know what you mean!

00:06:33.319 --> 00:06:35.580
Literally, we don’t know what you mean.

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:40.210
People, shops, homes, offices, entire cities
just quietly erased.

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:43.990
Imagine crowds of people just sliding past
each other on the street,

00:06:43.990 --> 00:06:50.050
just by unspoken, unknown, mutual consensus,
just removing each other.

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:54.129
I mean, China Miéville talked about this
in his book ‘The City &amp; the City’, right?

00:06:54.129 --> 00:06:57.970
But those two cities were divided by tradition,
and etiquette,

00:06:57.970 --> 00:07:00.090
and occasional violent retribution,

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:02.740
and tourists just had to fit in – there’d
be none of that.

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:03.740
Instead of going to war,

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:05.810
feuding nations could just block each other.

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:09.780
And their citizens’ implants
would be legally mandated

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:13.940
to just quietly edit the other country out.

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:19.280
Maybe it gets to the point where you don’t
know the other world exists

00:07:19.289 --> 00:07:20.630
until you're an adult.

00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:22.090
Maybe children are just kept from it,

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:23.850
because you don’t want to
talk to those people.

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:24.850
Until then it’s just a rumour,

00:07:24.850 --> 00:07:27.979
it’s just bumps in the night and explanations
for ghost stories.

00:07:27.979 --> 00:07:30.540
And then you get to being 18, or 21, or whatever,

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:33.020
and you have a look in the other world to
see what it’s like,

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:34.820
and it’s terrible.

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:37.140
Never ever want to go back there.

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:38.970
They’ll say there are people
who've blocked everyone,

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:40.949
and we’ll never know for sure.

00:07:40.949 --> 00:07:43.569
But I could look out at a
full house like this

00:07:43.569 --> 00:07:47.319
and not know if my eyes are gliding over the
people from another world.

00:07:47.319 --> 00:07:51.139
Maybe there’s someone else on stage and
I'm just walking and talking around them,

00:07:51.139 --> 00:07:54.669
because that’s what my implant lets me do.

00:07:54.669 --> 00:07:57.629
Which means there is
one little question left over:

00:07:57.629 --> 00:08:03.980
what happens when you edit out the idea of
implants from people who have implants?

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:05.480
Because there are going to be people who hate
the idea.

00:08:05.490 --> 00:08:06.949
Maybe they were fitted with them from birth.

00:08:06.949 --> 00:08:10.430
Maybe they were given them for work and have
to have them, but they hate the idea

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:16.229
that the thing that is heading through their
eyes into their brain isn’t reality.

00:08:16.229 --> 00:08:20.419
And how would that be any different from me
being on this stage right now

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:25.100
telling a story and you immediately dismissing
this as science fiction?

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:29.440
Ignore that itch behind your ear,
it’s probably nothing.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:31.560
My name’s Tom Scott, have a good night.

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:36.600
MATT PARKER: All right!

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:39.260
There's your camera...

00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:41.620
Tom Scott, ladies and gentlemen!

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:43.880
My goodness.

